// Thirty-two bytes out of a hash — and the question of where that type lives.
//
// Written because three packages named a file in this one that nobody had written:
// `@/packages/tls/src/keyschedule.wac` names three, `@/packages/ssz/src/chunk.wac` names
// `sha256.wac`, and `@/packages/tor/src/onionaddr.wac` names `keccak.wac`. The rewrite of this
// package had covered `secret.wac` and stopped, because that is where the language question was.
//
// Writing the digests found a second one, and it is about ownership rather than about a type.
//
// ## Four packages declare their own fixed-width wrapper
//
//   * `@/packages/ssz/src/chunk.wac` — `Chunk`, because SSZ is defined over 32-byte chunks;
//   * `@/packages/ens/src/answer.wac` — `EthAddress`, twenty rather than thirty-two, same shape;
//   * `@/packages/bls/src/fp.wac` — `Fp`, a field element of fixed width held as `u32[] limbs`;
//   * this file — `Digest32`.
//
// **Two counts, and confusing them is why five files had five different numbers.** *Declaring a
// wrapper* is four packages; *wanting the length in the type* is six, and the extra two —
// `@/packages/raster` and `@/packages/tor` — ask for it without declaring anything, because their
// values are a tile's pixels and two checksum bytes and neither is worth a struct on its own.
// `../../../QUESTIONS.md` owns both lists.
//
// (An earlier version of this list had `@/packages/mpt` in it. Its node hash is a plain `Bytes` and
// it declares no wrapper and makes no request; the citation came from another file's list, which had
// it wrong too.)
//
// Each of the four is a struct wrapping a `Bytes` whose length was checked once, and each has the
// same footnote. That is one language request, filed once, and it is **not** what this file is
// about.
//
// ## The question this file is about: is a digest a chunk?
//
// `Chunk.of` checks a length. `sha256` answers 32 bytes and cannot do otherwise, so
// `@/packages/ssz` had to write
//
//     Chunk unchecked(Bytes b) { … }
//
// with a comment saying *this declares the check away, once, in the one place where the fact is
// known*. If `sha256` answered a `Digest32` the check would not need declaring away — and `Chunk`
// still could not simply **be** `Digest32`, because most chunks are not digests. A chunk is a
// unit of merkleization; a leaf chunk is padded user data and has never been hashed.
//
// So they are two types with one representation, one of which is produced by the other, and the
// conversion cannot fail. Three positions, and this file takes the first:
//
//   1. **Two types and an infallible conversion.** `Chunk.ofDigest(d)` takes a `Digest32` and needs
//      no `Result`, beside `Chunk.of(Bytes)` which does. Honest, and it is one more member on a type
//      that already has four.
//   2. **One type in `core`.** `Slice<u8, 32>` if it existed, and then neither package declares
//      anything — at the cost of `Chunk` and `Digest32` and `EthAddress`'s twenty being the same
//      type again, which is the check they were introduced to get.
//   3. **`Digest32` in `core` and every other one on top of it.** Wrong on its face: a digest is not
//      more fundamental than a chunk, it is just the one that happens to be produced by a function
//      everybody calls.
//
// The reason the first is right *here* is that the conversion is where the meaning changes — *these
// bytes are now a leaf* — and a named conversion is the only place to write that down.

import { Bytes } from "core";

/**
 * The output of a 256-bit hash.
 *
 * No constructor from bytes. The only way to get one is to hash something, which is the whole of
 * what the type is for: a value that is 32 bytes because of where it came from rather than because
 * somebody checked.
 */
export const struct Digest32 {
  Bytes bytes;

  /** Two digests, compared. `Slice` has no `eq` and this is the type that most wants one. */
  bool eq(const this, Digest32 other) { … }
}

// ## What could not be written
//
// **`Digest32` is constructible by anyone in the same file, and there is no way to say otherwise.**
// `Digest32(someBytes)` is a struct construction and nothing marks the field private, so the
// no-constructor rule above is a convention held by this file being short. wac has no visibility
// inside a module — `export` is the only control and it is per declaration — so *a type only its own
// file may build* is not expressible. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
//
// That matters more here than in most places, because the guarantee is the point: a `Digest32` is
// trustworthy only if every one of them came out of a hash.
//
// **A 512-bit digest would be a second type, and then a third.** `sha512` answers 64 bytes and
// `Digest64` is the same file again with one number changed. `Digest<N>` is what a length in a type
// gives, and `../../../QUESTIONS.md` records the six packages asking for it — so this is one more
// voice for one request rather than a new question. The count is there rather than here because five
// files each carried their own and no two agreed.
